---@class archil.GrepDiskRequest
---@field id string Disk ID (format `dsk-{16 hex chars}`)
---@field directory string Directory on the disk to search, relative to the disk root. An empty string or "/" means the disk root.
---@field pattern string Regular expression in [ripgrep syntax](https://docs.rs/regex/latest/regex/#syntax). A newline separates alternative patterns, except with `multiline`, where it matches a line break.
---@field recursive? boolean When true, walks subdirectories breadth-first.
---@field maxDurationSeconds? number Wall-clock deadline for the entire request. Capped at 30 seconds because the runtime exec microVM itself is bounded at ~30s; longer requests would have their workers killed mid-scan.
---@field concurrency? number Maximum number of parallel grep workers. Higher values finish larger datasets within the deadline but consume proportionally more runtime capacity.
---@field maxResults? number Stop scanning once the aggregator has this many matches. Returned matches are a sample of whichever workers reported first, not the lexicographically first N.
---@field ignoreCase? boolean Match the pattern case-insensitively (`rg -i`). Defaults to false.
---@field multiline? boolean Let a match span lines (`rg -U`): `\n` or a newline character in the pattern matches a line break, and `.` matches one only after `(?s)`. Files with Windows line endings need `\r?\n` instead of `\n`. Matches on adjacent lines come back as one match covering all of them, with `endLine` set, and count once toward `maxResults`. A file with at least `maxResults` matching lines stops the request with `max_results`, even when merging leaves fewer matches. Defaults to false.
---@field globs? archil.GrepDiskRequestGlobsList Path globs with ripgrep's `-g` rules, matched against paths relative to `directory`. A leading `!` excludes. A glob without a `/` matches a file or directory name at any depth; a glob with a `/` matches the whole relative path, and a leading `/` anchors it to `directory`. A trailing `/` matches only directories, so use `dir/**` to search the files under `dir`. When several globs match a path, the last one wins. A directory excluded this way is skipped with everything under it. If any non-`!` glob is given, files that match no glob are skipped. A file a glob matches is searched or skipped regardless of `types` and `typesNot`.
---@field types? archil.GrepDiskRequestTypesList Only search files of these types, using ripgrep's default type names (for example `py`, `ts`, `go`, `md`).
---@field typesNot? archil.GrepDiskRequestTypesNotList Skip files of these types, using ripgrep's default type names. Takes precedence over `types`.
---@field contextBefore? number Lines of context to return before each match (`rg -B`). When either context field is set, the result includes `groups`.
---@field contextAfter? number Lines of context to return after each match (`rg -A`). When either context field is set, the result includes `groups`.

